{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_gbgzesonzpnzsveps43y7hopwiryd7or7tu32774ze6ilnvsnrkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## Scope enumeration (definition of done)\n\nI built the C library via its own `CMakeLists.txt` and ran `nm -D` on the resulting `.so`. The library is genuinely small — 3 files, 49 lines total — and exports exactly **one** public symbol:\n\n```\nto_barycentric        # the only T (defined, global) symbol; rest are weak libc stubs\n```\n\n`lm_v2`, `lm_sub2`, `lm_dot2` are `static`, so they are correctly *not* exported. No namespace/renaming macros exist in `include/lib.h`, so the linker name equals the source name.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- `Cargo.toml` — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`\n- `src/lib.rs` — `#[repr(C)] lm_vec2`, plus `to_barycentric` as `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\"`\n\n**Symbol diff between the C `.so` and Rust `.so` is empty** — identical export sets.\n\n## The one substantive finding\n\nA naive line-by-line translation compiled and passed ordinary inputs, but my differential harness found **307 bit-level mismatches** — all NaN *sign-bit* differences (C `7fc00000` vs Rust `ffc00000`).\n\nRoot cause: on x86, `mulss/addss/subss` return the **destination** operand (quieted) when it is NaN, so when both operands are NaN the destination decides the sign — and x86 manufactures the negative \"indefinite\" QNaN `0xFFC00000` for invalid ops like `0*inf`. LLVM freely commutes operands; GCC at `-O0` does not, and notably is *not* uniformly left-as-destination. In `lm_dot2` (`a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y`) the reference codegen uses the **right** operand as destination for both the `y` product and the addition:\n\n```\nmulss %xmm0,%xmm1   ; a.x * b.x  -> dest = a.x   (left)\nmulss %xmm2,%xmm0   ; b.y * a.y  -> dest = b.y   (RIGHT)\naddss %xmm1,%xmm0   ; y-term + x-term -> dest = y-term (RIGHT)\n```\n\nI therefore route the arithmetic through `mul_ss/add_ss/sub_ss/div_ss` primitives that pin the destination operand via inline `asm!`, mirroring the reference instruction-for-instruction (with a portable fallback for non-x86). No bugs were \"fixed\" — the unguarded divide by a zero determinant still yields inf/NaN exactly as in C.\n\n## Verification (my own commands, not self-reports)\n\n| Test | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Special-value sweep (11⁴ combos of ±0, ±1, ±inf, NaN, subnormal, ±FLT_MAX) + 400k random | **0 mismatches** |\n| NaN/inf/zero-heavy stress, 3,000,000 cases, random NaN payloads & signs | **0 mismatches** |\n| 1,000,000 random finite inputs (935k produced NaN results) | C`-O0` = C`-O2` = Rust, **0 mismatches** |\n| Rust **debug** build vs C | 0 mismatches on both suites |\n\nOne caveat worth stating: the C library's own `-O2` build differs from its `-O0` build on 354 of these NaN-input cases, so no single binary can match both. I matched the build the repo actually defines (`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, hence unoptimized). For every input that is not itself a NaN/inf, all three binaries agree, so that choice only matters for NaN-valued inputs."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1426,"cache_read_input_tokens":46606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1426,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"abe49327-4f81-4276-8618-76580ee9dc2a","uuid":"af55433a-bc6e-47d0-9ac5-5f1ff489d70e","timestamp":"2026-08-19T10:39:24.373Z"}
